React re-renders a child even with identical-looking props usually because of one of these:

1. **New reference every render (most common)** — if the parent passes an inline object, array, or function as a prop, it's a *new* reference each render, even if the values are the same:
   ```jsx
   <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />        // new object every time
   <Child onClick={() => doThing()} />        // new function every time
   <Child items={[1, 2, 3]} />                 // new array every time
   ```
   `React.memo` does a shallow comparison, so these always look "different."

2. **Child isn't memoized at all** — by default, a component re-renders whenever its parent re-renders, regardless of props. Wrap it in `React.memo(Child)` to opt into shallow prop comparison.

3. **Context changes** — if `Child` (or something inside it) consumes a Context, any change to that context value triggers a re-render, independent of props.

4. **Parent re-renders trigger children by default** — even with `React.memo`, if a non-primitive prop changes reference, memo won't help unless you fix #1.

## Fix checklist

```jsx
// 1. Memoize the child
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style, onClick, items }) {
  ...
});

// 2. Stabilize object/array props
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red' }), []);
const items = useMemo(() => [1, 2, 3], []);

// 3. Stabilize function props
const onClick = useCallback(() => doThing(), []);
```

If you paste the parent/child component code, I can pinpoint exactly which prop is getting a new reference.
